# Changelog
All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.

## [3.5.0] - Floating point delays; right-click no longer deletes points
### Added:
- Move delay periods now accept floating point seconds: decimals use a dot, e.g. `1.5`, `1.5, 2.5` or `1.5-3` (ranges step by 1 from the start value). Works in both the HUD delay field and the Patrol Menu. Commas remain the list separator (`1,5` = the two delays 1s and 5s), never a decimal separator. In the HUD delay field, invalid input (letters, a second dot, empty) reverts to the previous value when you click out of the field; the Patrol Menu keeps the stored delays on invalid input.
### Removed:
- Right-clicking a patrol point no longer deletes it (too easy to hit by accident). Left-click the point and choose **Delete point** in the popup instead; right-click on the canvas is left entirely to Foundry's own context menus.
### Fixed:
- Delay ranges no longer duplicate the range's end value (`2-4` produced 2,3,4,4; now 2,3,4 as documented).

## [3.4.1] - Bug fix
### Fixed:
- Fixed patrol points not being selectable/editable with the mouse on displays where the canvas backing store is denser than its CSS size (`devicePixelRatio != 1` — HiDPI screens, Windows display scaling, browser zoom e.g. in Firefox). `clientToWorld()` in `module/coords.js` converted client px to canvas bitmap px but did not divide by `renderer.resolution`, the unit `stage.toLocal()` expects (PIXI v8's `EventSystem.mapPositionToPoint` does divide). The computed world point was off by the pixel-ratio factor, so the DOM-level point hit-test missed every marker and clicks did nothing. The conversion now mirrors PIXI's own event mapping exactly (byte-identical behavior at resolution 1).
- Fixed the previous scene's patrol points showing up on a newly viewed scene after an in-place scene switch (no page reload; observed stacked at an arbitrary spot in Firefox). The patrol `tokenMap` was loaded once at `ready` and never reloaded, so the next `livePlotUpdate()` (e.g. selecting a token via `controlToken`) projected the stale previous-scene data into the new scene's `routes` flag and the layer rendered it. `canvasReady` now reloads the tokenMap from the viewed scene (`TokenPatrollerManager.reloadFromScene()`), halts patrol loops still bound to the old scene without persisting them, and re-syncs + redraws the layer. As defense-in-depth, `_saveAndUpdate()`/`_syncRoutesFlag()` skip the flag write when the viewed scene differs from the scene the tokenMap was loaded from (`_ownsViewedScene()`), so one scene's patrol data can never be written into another scene's flags.
- Point picking no longer drops every press when `canvas.tokens.hover` is stale (e.g. left over from the previous scene before the first pointermove after a scene switch): a destroyed or no-longer-present hover is ignored and the geometric token check applies instead. Presses on live tokens are still left entirely to the token layer.

## [3.4.0] - Bug fix
### Fixed:
- Fixed patrol point visuals (the dashed route line and point circles) staying on the map after deleting all of a token's points (HUD trash dialog, popup delete, or right-click): Foundry's `setFlag` diff-merges objects, so the token's stale entry in the scene `routes` render-cache flag was never removed — the layer kept drawing it while the underlying `plots` were empty, leaving dead, unselectable circles. `saveRoutes()` in `module/store.js` now unsets the `routes` flag before writing it (and skips the set entirely when the projection is empty), so the layer redraws with the deleted route gone.
- **Migration:** on world load, the module now recomputes the `routes` render cache of **every scene in the world** from that scene's `patrols` flag (the source of truth). Scenes saved before this fix are cleaned up automatically — only stale entries (tokens whose points were all deleted, or tokens that were deleted) are removed; existing routes are re-written identically. `_syncRoutesFlag()` and the migration skip the flag write when the cache already matches, so the migration costs nothing once a scene is clean.

### Removed:
- Removed the "Clear Routes" keyboard shortcut (Ctrl+Shift+D) and its `clearAllRoutes` handler from `module/keys.js`. Deleting routes remains available via the HUD trash dialog and per-point deletion.

## [3.3.1] - Bug fix
### Fixed:
- Sidebar Patrol Tool buttons did not react on click: in Foundry v13/v14 a scene-control tool's `onClick` only fires on an actual button click when the tool is flagged `button: true`. Without it the click only moved `activeTool`, so start/stop applied late (only after clicking another control) and the "Routes started/halted" notification popped up when merely opening the Patrol Tool or clicking another tool afterwards. The tools in `module/scene-control.js` now set `button: true` and the control keeps `activeTool: ""`, so start/stop apply immediately on click and the notification only appears when the GM actually presses one.

## [3.3.0] - Patrol Tool scene control (start/stop all patrols)
### Added:
- New **Patrol Tool** scene control on the left sidebar (route icon, GM only) with two buttons: **Start all patrols** (play icon) and **Stop all patrols** (stop icon). They run the exact same actions as the Ctrl+Shift+G / Ctrl+Shift+H keybindings — start/halt the patrols of the selected tokens, or of all owned tokens when nothing is selected — so the GM no longer needs the keyboard shortcuts. Implemented in the new `module/scene-control.js`, which reuses the exported `startAllRoutes`/`haltAllRoutes` handlers from `keys.js`. Point picking/editing on the map stays DOM-level and always available, independent of this control.
### Changed:
- `registerPatrolControl()` in `module/scene-control.js` split into single-responsibility pieces: a `PATROL_TOOLS` data table (name/title/icon/handler, like `KEYBINDINGS` in `keys.js`), `buildTools()` and `buildPatrolControl()` builders (a fresh control object per hook call, since Foundry may mutate what it receives), `addSceneControl()` for the v13 Record-vs-array insertion, and `registerPatrolControl()` reduced to just hook wiring + the GM guard. No behavior change.

## [3.2.5] - Internal refactor (no functionality change)
### Changed:
- Refactored the modules under `module/` for single-responsibility and less duplication, with no behavior change: the walking loop moved from `patroller.js` to `patrol-engine.js`, grid-path math to `linear-routes.js`, delay-string parsing to `delay.js`, the per-token data schema to `schema.js`, DOM/PIXI event coordinate conversion to `coords.js`, PIXI v7/v8 graphics helpers to `pixi-compat.js`, and DOM-level point picking to `point-interaction.js`. `store.js` is now the single owner of the scene flags, `patroller.js` keeps the public `TP.tokenPatroller` API and only coordinates (mutate -> persist -> redraw), and the ~16 identical one-line getters are generated from a table. `hud.js`/`menu.js`/`keys.js`/`vision.js`/`speech.js`/`controls.js`/`config.js` were de-duplicated (button factories, tab/setting tables, shared helpers). Verified by syntax-checking every file plus a mocked behavioral harness: identical side-effect logs between the old and new code across PIXI v8 and v7 drawing paths. Not run in a live Foundry client.

## [3.2.4] - Bug fix
### Bug Fixes:
- Fixed dragging a token that sits on top of a patrol point also selecting and dragging the point. The DOM-level point picking in `routes-layer.js` now drops any canvas `pointerdown` whose scene position is inside a token — `tokenAtWorldPoint()` checks `canvas.tokens.hover` first, then falls back to a geometric hit test (`token.bounds.contains`, else the token document's grid-cell rectangle) while the token layer is the active layer. Presses on tokens are left entirely to Foundry (select / drag / context menu), so a point under the token is never selected, moved, or deleted. Left-click / drag / right-click on a bare point still work as before, regardless of the active tool.

## [3.2.3] - Bug fix
### Bug Fixes:
- Fixed clicking a patrol point circle still doing nothing (it never worked, with or without the Patrol Tool). Point picking no longer relies on PIXI layer hit-testing at all (layer eventMode/interactiveChildren/active-layer behavior made that unreliable across Foundry v13/v14 builds): a single `pointerdown` listener on the canvas element now converts the click to scene coordinates via the stage transform and hit-tests every plotted point directly. Left-click a point circle to get the options popup (**Delete point** / **Move point**, plus a drag hint), drag the point to move it (grid-snapped, persisted on release via `movePlotPoint`), right-click to delete it, click empty space or press Escape to dismiss. Works for GMs regardless of the active canvas layer/tool. The non-functional Patrol Tool scene control was removed.

## [3.2.2] - Bug fix
### Bug Fixes:
- Fixed patrol points still not being selectable/editable with the mouse on Foundry v13/v14, despite 3.2.1. Root cause: under PIXI v8, a canvas layer subtree is pruned from hit-testing when the layer is `"passive"` with `interactiveChildren === false` (the `CanvasLayer` default in v13), and a plain container without a `hitArea` is never a hit target itself — so the routes layer never received pointer events and the layer-level fallback added in 3.2.1 was dead code. Edit mode now gives the layer a full-scene `hitArea`, sets `eventMode = "static"` and `interactiveChildren = true`, and re-asserts all of that after every layer redraw (canvas draws can reset layer flags), so clicking a point marker reliably selects it (ring + options popup), dragging moves it (snapped, persisted via `movePlotPoint`), and right-click deletes it. The Patrol Tool scene control now also provides the `order` field required by the v13/v14 control schema, selection is cleaned up on Escape/empty clicks and when a redraw removes the selected point, and pointer coordinates are read through v8 (`event.global`/`event.client`) with v7 (`event.data`) fallbacks.

## [3.2.1] - Bug fix
### Bug Fixes:
- Fixed plot points never being selectable/editable with the Patrol Tool (the sidebar button appeared, but clicks on points did nothing): the patrol scene control is now associated with its layer (`layer: "patrolRoutes"`) so Foundry activates the routes layer when the tool is selected and dispatches canvas pointer events to it. Additionally, edit mode now makes the layer itself interactive and hit-tests points at the layer level (`RoutesLayer._handleLayerPointerDown` + `RouteDrawing.hitTestMarker`), so selection/drag/delete work even when per-object PIXI hit testing doesn't reach the markers; marker handlers call `stopPropagation()` to avoid double handling.

## [3.2.0] - Patrol Tool scene control
### Added:
- New **Patrol Tool** scene control on the left toolbar (route icon, GM only) with an "Edit patrol points" toggle. While active, plotted point markers are interactive: left-click a point to select it (a small options popup offers **Delete point**), **drag** the point on the map to move it (snapped to the grid unless gridless), or right-click a point to delete it immediately. A selected point is highlighted with a white ring. While the tool is inactive, markers no longer intercept canvas clicks.
- New API method `TP.tokenPatroller.movePlotPoint(tokenId, index, {x, y})` used by the drag-and-drop (persists the moved point, rebuilds linear routes when in linear mode, and redraws).

## [3.1.2] - Bug fix
### Bug Fixes:
- Fixed worlds still failing to load with `marker.fill is not a function` after 3.1.1: the PIXI build in the affected Foundry versions also lacks the v8 chainable `fill()`/`stroke()` styling (v7-style `lineStyle()`/`beginFill()`/`endFill()` only). Marker dots and the dashed route line are now drawn through whichever API the runtime provides (`circle()`/`drawShape()`/`drawCircle()`/`arc()`/path segments, styled via `fill()`/`stroke()` or `lineStyle()`/`beginFill()`/`endFill()`), and marker interactivity sets both `eventMode` (v8) and `interactive` (v7).

## [3.1.1] - Bug fix
### Bug Fixes:
- Fixed worlds failing to load with `marker.circle is not a function`: the 3.1.0 plot-point markers called the `Graphics#circle()` helper, which is not available on the PIXI build in the affected Foundry versions and threw uncaught during the routes layer draw. Markers are now drawn via `drawShape(new PIXI.Circle(...))`, falling back to `arc()` or a line-segment path when those are unavailable.

## [3.0.0] - Foundry VTT V13/V14 modernization
### Changed:
- Migrated the module to Foundry VTT V13+ / V14: modern `module.json` manifest with `esmodules` and a `compatibility` block (minimum 13, verified 14)
- Rewrote the codebase as ES modules under `module/` (was a single legacy script)
- Replaced the vendored Azzurite settings-extender with core keybindings (`game.keybindings.register`), configurable in the Controls settings
- Patrol data is now stored in the viewed scene's module flags instead of a journal entry file
- Token data access updated to the V10+ document model (`token.document`, `token.x/y`, `canvas.grid.type`, ...)
- Custom routes layer is registered through `CONFIG.Canvas.layers` instead of monkey-patching `Canvas.layers`
- Route rendering updated to PIXI v8 (`stroke()` instead of the removed `lineStyle()`)
- Token movement waits on `Token#movementAnimationPromise`; token rotation uses `atan2` so tokens face their direction of travel
- Vision checks use the token's own vision source (FOV + wall-limited LOS) with a manual range/angle/wall fallback
- Ambient audio uses `AmbientSound` documents created via `scene.createEmbeddedDocuments` (`walls` flag for local audio)
- Combat creation uses `Combat.create` / `createEmbeddedDocuments("Combatant", ...)`
- Token HUD buttons are injected with native DOM (V13+ renders the HUD with ApplicationV2 and passes an HTMLElement to `renderTokenHUD`)
- Chat messages set the Polyglot language through message flags instead of manipulating the chat input DOM

### Removed:
- Dropped the bundled `config/settings-extender.js` (Azzu) dependency
- Dropped the journal-entry based patrol database (`PatrolDataManager` file structure)

